    source-map symbolication, alerting, retention. Costs a runtime dependency and a vendor, and error payloads
    (including URLs and any context you attach) leave your infra. Rules out nothing technically, but vendor SDKs tend to
    sprawl into the code that imports them, so the seam below matters.
  - [1mYour own collect endpoint[0m — you POST batched JSON to something you own. No vendor, no data egress, full control of
    retention. Costs real work that is [3mnot in this repo[0m: there's no backend here at all — [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m in [38;5;153mapp.js:2[39m
    points at [38;5;153mapi.example.com[39m, a stub. You'd be signing up to build and operate a receiver, plus storage and some way to
    search it.
  - [1mTransport seam now, destination later[0m — build the logger against a small transport interface, ship a console
    transport, wire a real sink when you pick one. Cheap and fully reversible, but on its own it does [1mnot[0m solve the
    stated problem. Silent errors stay silent until a real sink exists.

  [1mMy recommendation: hosted service, behind a transport seam we own.[0m The seam is not an alternative to picking a
  destination — it's how we keep the vendor out of [38;5;153mapp.js[39m and keep the logger testable without network calls. I'd build
  the seam in every version of this design; the question is only what sits behind it on day one. I recommend the hosted
  service because your problem is "I never find out," and buying alerting and dedup outright beats building a receiver
  for an app this size.

  One caveat that applies to all three: the login handler has a password in scope and currently logs the username.
  Whichever destination we pick, the design needs a redaction rule at the logger boundary — a hard allowlist of what may
  be attached to an event — so a future careless [38;5;153mlogger.error("login failed", formData)[39m can't exfiltrate a credential.
  That's a design constraint I'll write in regardless of your answer here.
